    [
        'slug' => 'dancheong-the-five-colors-of-korean-palaces', 'category' => 'culture', 'cover' => ['dancheong', '#f6e6e0'], 'author' => 'Haneul Park', 'days_ago' => 9, 'featured' => false,
        'tags' => ['dancheong', 'palace', 'colors'], 'products' => ['dancheong-rosette-oversized-tee', 'hanji-window-throw-cushion'],
        'en' => ['title' => 'Dancheong: the five colors of Korean palaces', 'excerpt' => 'Blue, red, yellow, white and black — the paint on every palace eave is a map of the universe.', 'content' => "Look up at the eaves of Gyeongbokgung and you will see a riot of patterns. That is **dancheong**, literally “cinnabar and blue-green”, a painting tradition that protects wood and tells a story.\n\n## The five directions\n- **Blue** — east, wood and spring\n- **Red** — south, fire and summer\n- **Yellow** — center, earth\n- **White** — west, metal and autumn\n- **Black** — north, water and winter\n\n## More than decoration\nThe paint seals timber against rain and insects, while the patterns — lotus, peonies, geometric bands — were believed to chase away bad luck. Palaces use the richest palettes; temples and homes use quieter ones.\n\n> Color was architecture’s first language, long before signs and logos.\n\n## On your wardrobe\nOur Dancheong Rosette tee distills a single palace flower into a chest-sized print, while the Hanji cushion borrows the calm lattice of hanok windows."],
        'fr' => ['title' => 'Dancheong : les cinq couleurs des palais coréens', 'excerpt' => 'Bleu, rouge, jaune, blanc et noir — la peinture des avant-toits est une carte de l\'univers.', 'content' => "Levez les yeux vers les avant-toits de Gyeongbokgung : une explosion de motifs. C'est le **dancheong**, littéralement « cinabre et bleu-vert », une tradition picturale qui protège le bois et raconte une histoire.\n\n## Les cinq directions\n- **Bleu** — l'est, le bois, le printemps\n- **Rouge** — le sud, le feu, l'été\n- **Jaune** — le centre, la terre\n- **Blanc** — l'ouest, le métal, l'automne\n- **Noir** — le nord, l'eau, l'hiver\n\n## Plus qu'un décor\nLa peinture protège le bois de la pluie et des insectes, tandis que les motifs — lotus, pivoines, bandes géométriques — étaient censés éloigner le mauvais sort. Les palais emploient les palettes les plus riches ; temples et maisons, des teintes plus sobres.\n\n> La couleur fut le premier langage de l'architecture, bien avant les enseignes et les logos.\n\n## Dans votre garde-robe\nNotre t-shirt Rosace Dancheong condense une fleur de palais en impression de poitrine, et le coussin Hanji emprunte le calme des treillis de hanok."],
        'ko' => ['title' => '단청: 한국 궁궐의 다섯 가지 색', 'excerpt' => '청·적·황·백·흑 — 처마의 색은 우주의 지도예요.', 'content' => "경복궁 처마를 올려다보면 화려한 무늬가 가득해요. 바로 **단청**, 나무를 보호하고 이야기를 전하는 채색 전통이에요.\n\n## 다섯 방위의 색\n- **청** — 동쪽, 나무, 봄\n- **적** — 남쪽, 불, 여름\n- **황** — 중앙, 흙\n- **백** — 서쪽, 쇠, 가을\n- **흑** — 북쪽, 물, 겨울\n\n## 장식 그 이상\n단청은 비와 벌레로부터 목재를 지키고, 연꽃·모란·기하 무늬는 액운을 막는다고 믿었어요. 궁궐은 가장 화려한 색을, 사찰과 살림집은 차분한 색을 썼어요.\n\n> 간판과 로고가 생기기 훨씬 전, 색은 건축의 첫 번째 언어였어요.\n\n## 옷장 속으로\n단청 로제트 티셔츠는 궁궐의 꽃 한 송이를 가슴 프린트로, 한지 쿠션은 한옥 창살의 고요함을 담았어요."],
